  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14210035/assets/d8788f03a4cb07d8f447346699ed5a9e8231cc4e496e135b6df1515e56792d61.png)\n\nThe graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14210035/assets/ddf15053fee30ed69e2caed1a049b34c6f39c729fec0f1a053b00e33686c1e5f.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,6) & ⟶(6,0) \\\\ (1,4) & ⟶(4,1) \\\\ (2,-2) & ⟶(-2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
